---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import { installTabs } from '../data/commands';
---
<Base title="Install Heimdall" description="Install Heimdall with one command or a .deb package. Requirements, what it changes, how to verify, upgrade and uninstall." current="Install">
  <div class="wrap hero" style="padding-bottom:8px">
    <div class="eyebrow">Install</div>
    <h1>One command.<br>Your machine, your data.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Heimdall runs on a Linux machine on your home network, ideally wired. Pick a tier, run the command, open the dashboard.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap">
    <Terminal id="install" tabs={installTabs} />
    <p class="faint small">Tiers: <code>minimal</code> | <code>intermediate</code> | <code>full</code>. Add <code>--router=passive</code> if your router is not listed. Then open <code>http://&lt;this-machine&gt;:3000</code>: the first visit asks you to create your admin account.</p>
    <div class="callout caution"><strong>Do not expose it</strong>Never forward ports 3000 or 5000 to the internet. Heimdall is for your LAN. Reach it remotely through a VPN you already trust.</div>
  </div>
  <section><div class="wrap"><h2>Choose a tier</h2><div class="grid g3">
    <div class="card"><div class="meta">Tier · minimal</div><h3>Core stack</h3><p class="muted">Database, cache, backend and dashboard. The passive detectors, ML anomaly detection, Nmap, threat intel, block and quarantine.</p></div>
    <div class="card info"><div class="meta">Tier · intermediate</div><h3>+ Honeypot</h3><p class="muted">Adds OpenCanary: fake FTP, Telnet, SSH and HTTP services. Anything that touches one is a critical alert naming the source.</p></div>
    <div class="card warn"><div class="meta">Tier · full</div><h3>+ Zeek, ClamAV, Suricata</h3><p class="muted">Adds DNS and connection analysis, file scanning and a signature IDS. Needs about 4 GB of RAM.</p></div>
  </div></div></section>
  <section><div class="wrap"><h2>Requirements</h2><div class="tablewrap"><table><tbody>
    <tr><td class="k">Machine</td><td>Linux on the same LAN as the devices you want to watch. A wired port is strongly preferred; WiFi sniffing is limited.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Software</td><td>Docker with the compose plugin. The script can add it (<code>--install-docker</code>); the .deb pulls it in.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Router</td><td>Optional. Pick yours on the Equipment tab, or choose “Other / not listed” and Heimdall works from its own view of the network.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Architecture</td><td>x86-64 today. ARM images are not published yet.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div></div></section>
  <section><div class="wrap"><h2>What the installer changes</h2><div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card"><h3>It does</h3><ul class="muted tight"><li>Create <code>~/heimdall</code> (or <code>/opt/heimdall</code> for the .deb) with the compose file and config.</li><li>Generate secrets unique to this machine in <code>secrets/</code>, readable only by you. It never asks for, or writes, your dashboard password.</li><li>Write <code>.env</code> with your tier, interface and subnet.</li><li>Pull images and start the containers (the .deb waits until you run <code>sudo heimdall start</code>).</li></ul></div>
    <div class="card crit"><h3>It does not</h3><ul class="muted tight"><li>Touch your router’s settings or password.</li><li>Open any port to the internet.</li><li>Send your network data anywhere. Threat-intel lookups send an address to a public feed only when you ask for one.</li></ul></div>
  </div></div></section>
  <section id="verify"><div class="wrap"><h2>Verify before you run</h2>
    <p class="muted">Piping a script to a shell asks for trust. Here is how to need less of it.</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><div><strong>Read the script.</strong> <a href="https://github.com/danielonet/heimdall/blob/main/install/get-heimdall.sh">install/get-heimdall.sh</a> is the file the command downloads.</div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Check the checksum.</strong> Every release download has a published SHA-256 next to it.
        <div class="term"><div class="cmd"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>curl -fsSLO https://heimdall.it.com/download/heimdall_all.deb.sha256{'\n'}<span class="p">$ </span>sha256sum -c heimdall_all.deb.sha256{'\n'}<span class="c">heimdall_all.deb: OK</span></pre></div></div></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Use the manual route.</strong> The “By hand” tab does the same thing one step at a time.</div></li>
    </ol>
  </div></section>
  <section><div class="wrap"><div class="grid g2">
    <div><h2>Upgrade</h2><p class="muted">Script: run the same command again. Secrets and <code>.env</code> are kept and the image version moves up. .deb: install the newer .deb. Database migrations run on start.</p></div>
    <div><h2>Uninstall</h2><p class="muted">Stop it with <code>sudo heimdall stop</code>, then <code>sudo apt remove heimdall</code>. Your data and secrets stay. To delete everything: <code>sudo heimdall reset</code> (asks first).</p></div>
  </div></div></section>
</Base>
